  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1213 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nTuesday through Monday:\n\nLow pressure deepens in the Canadian Prairies on Tuesday, with \nbroad, strengthening WAA in all levels. Frontogenesis at the \nlow levels will allow for precipitation development across \nsouthwestern to central Wisconsin, spreading eastward through \nthe afternoon. PWATs around 2 inches combined with mid-level \nMUCAPE in the 400-700 J/kg range and a broad area of lift, heavy\nrain becomes a threat going into the afternoon and overnight \nhours. West to east bulk shear of 35 to 40 kt and 850 mb LLJ \nnosing into northern Illinois overnight may allow for a period \nof storm intensification. The primary hazard looks to be urban \nand small stream flooding, but cannot rule out brief downburst \nactivity due to precipitation loading. Southwesterly winds \ngusting between 20 and 25 mph are expected even outside of \nstorms as low pressure continues to deepen. With gusty winds and\nbuilding waves Tuesday into Tuesday night, a moderate to high \nswim risk will also develop in Ozaukee and Sheboygan County Lake\nMichigan beaches.\n\nLow pressure lifts into the Hudson Bay Wednesday morning, the \nlast waves of rain and storms will progress southeastward out of \nWisconsin. The cold front will finally work its way through \nWednesday afternoon, bringing an end to any further thunderstorm \nactivity. Northwesterly winds develop after storms exit, with\ntemperatures falling into the low 70s quickly after the frontal\npassage. High pressure will build through the northern Plains into\nthe central Plains Wednesday afternoon through Wednesday night,\nbringing cool and dry conditions with lows in the 50s Wednesday\nnight and highs in the mid-70s Thursday. High pressure gradually\npropagates eastward through the Ohio Valley through Friday,\nkeeping conditions dry and highs in the upper 70s. \n\nAn active pattern returns into the weekend as multiple shortwaves\nswing southeastward through southern Canada and interact with lee\ncyclogenesis in the central to northern Great Plains. On and off\nperiods of thunderstorms are expected, with some potential for\nsevere weather if ingredients come together at the right time.\nActive pattern continues into early next week.\n\nMH",
